id: ERR-001
ilities: [analysability]
source: cpm
maturity: 3
action: advisory
title: Error checked but ignored (empty `if err != nil` block)
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: block-absence
skip_comments: true
skip_strings: true
target:
  extensions: .go
  exclude_paths: vendor/ .git/ build/ dist/ _test.go
  content_contains: err != nil
# A Go idiom smell: the code checks `if err != nil { ... }` but the block body
# does nothing meaningful with the error — no return, no log, no panic, no
# propagation. That silently swallows the failure and the program continues in
# a broken state. block_inner lists the things a real handler does; a checked-
# but-empty (or purely cosmetic) block is flagged. This is distinct from
# QUAL-050 (empty `catch {}` in JS/Java/etc.) which never matches Go's
# `if err != nil` construct. Sourced from the GitLab review-audit: "error
# handling" was the #2 topic reviewers raise by hand (49 comments).
patterns:
  - regex: \bif\s+err\s*!=\s*nil\s*\{
    message: "Error is checked but the `if err != nil` block does nothing with it — the failure is silently swallowed. Return it, wrap it, log it, or panic."
block_inner: \breturn\b|\bpanic\s*\(|\.Fatal|\.Error\s*\(|\.Errorf\s*\(|\blog\.|\bcontinue\b|\bbreak\b|os\.Exit\s*\(|\berr\s*=
fix: "Handle the error inside the block: `return fmt.Errorf(\"doing X: %w\", err)` at minimum. Reviewed exception: add `// cpm:ignore ERR-001` on the `if err != nil` line."
